Selected Idea
-------------

Project Gutenberg treats public-domain works as freely usable but protects its name and redistribution terms
Project Gutenberg makes the Gettysburg Address (and other U.S. public-domain works) available for free, but its Full Project Gutenberg License imposes requirements for redistribution tied to the Project Gutenberg trademark and distribution practices - creating obligations for anyone who redistributes Gutenberg-marked files even when the underlying work is public domain.

Research
--------

### Facts

1. "This eBook is for the use of anyone anywhere in the United States and most other parts of the world at no cost and with almost no restrictions whatsoever. You may copy it, give it away or re-use it under the terms of the Project Gutenberg License included with this eBook or online at www.gutenberg.org." (opening paragraph of the eBook header)

2. "Creating the works from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright law means that no one owns a United States copyright in these works, so the Foundation (and you!) can copy and distribute it in the United States without permission and without paying copyright royalties." (paragraph following the text)

3. "Project Gutenberg is a registered trademark." (Section 1.B)

4. "The Project Gutenberg Literary Archive Foundation ("the Foundation" or PGLAF), owns a compilation copyright in the collection of Project Gutenberg electronic works." (Section 1.C)

5. "If an individual work is unprotected by U.S. copyright law and you are located in the United States, we do not claim a right to prevent you from copying, distributing, performing, displaying or creating derivative works based on the work as long as all references to Project Gutenberg are removed." (Section 1.C)

6. "The copyright laws of the place where you are located also govern what you can do with this work." (Section 1.D)

7. "If you are not located in the United States, you will have to check the laws of the country where you are located before using this eBook." (opening paragraph of the eBook header)

8. "The Foundation makes no representations concerning the copyright status of any work in any country other than the United States." (Section 1.D)

9. "The following sentence, with active links to, or other immediate access to, the full Project Gutenberg License must appear prominently whenever any copy of a Project Gutenberg work (any work on which the phrase 'Project Gutenberg' appears, or with which the phrase 'Project Gutenberg' is associated) is accessed, displayed, performed, viewed, copied or distributed:" followed by the full required sentence beginning "This eBook is for the use of anyone anywhere in the United States..." (Section 1.E.1)

10. "Do not unlink or detach or remove the full Project Gutenberg License terms from this work, or any files containing a part of this work or any other work associated with Project Gutenberg." (Section 1.E.4)

11. "Do not copy, display, perform, distribute or redistribute this electronic work, or any part of this electronic work, without prominently displaying the sentence set forth in paragraph 1.E.1 with active links or immediate access to the full terms of the Project Gutenberg License." (Section 1.E.5)

12. "You may convert to and distribute this work in any binary, compressed, marked up, nonproprietary or proprietary form, including any word processing or hypertext form. However, if you provide access to or distribute copies of a Project Gutenberg work in a format other than 'Plain Vanilla ASCII' or other format used in the official version posted on the official Project Gutenberg website (www.gutenberg.org), you must, at no additional cost, fee or expense to the user, provide a copy, a means of exporting a copy, or a means of obtaining a copy upon request, of the work in its original 'Plain Vanilla ASCII' or other form." (Section 1.E.6)

13. "Do not charge a fee for access to, viewing, displaying, performing, copying or distributing any Project Gutenberg works unless you comply with paragraph 1.E.8 or 1.E.9." (Section 1.E.7)

14. "You may charge a reasonable fee for copies of or providing access to or distributing Project Gutenberg electronic works provided that: You pay a royalty fee of 20% of the gross profits you derive from the use of Project Gutenberg works calculated using the method you already use to calculate your applicable taxes." (first bullet of Section 1.E.8)

15. "You provide a full refund of any money paid by a user who notifies you in writing (or by e-mail) within 30 days of receipt that s/he does not agree to the terms of the full Project Gutenberg License. You must require such a user to return or destroy all copies of the works possessed in a physical medium and discontinue all use of and all access to other copies of Project Gutenberg works." (second bullet of Section 1.E.8)

16. "You provide, in accordance with paragraph 1.F.3, a full refund of any money paid for a work or a replacement copy, if a defect in the electronic work is discovered and reported to you within 90 days of receipt of the work." (third bullet of Section 1.E.8)

17. "You comply with all other terms of this agreement for free distribution of Project Gutenberg works." (fourth bullet of Section 1.E.8)

18. "If an individual Project Gutenberg electronic work is posted with the permission of the copyright holder, the work can be copied and distributed to anyone in the United States without paying any fees or charges" is followed by the condition that "your use and distribution must comply with both paragraphs 1.E.1 through 1.E.7 and any additional terms imposed by the copyright holder." (Section 1.E.3)

19. "The Full Project Gutenberg License is included with this eBook." (stated in the eBook header: "You may copy it...under the terms of the Project Gutenberg License included with this eBook or online at www.gutenberg.org.")

20. "Project Gutenberg eBooks are often created from several printed editions, all of which are confirmed as not protected by copyright in the U.S. unless a copyright notice is included." (Section 5)

21. "Updated editions will replace the previous one-the old editions will be renamed." (statement near the end of the eBook)

22. "The Project Gutenberg Literary Archive Foundation is a non-profit 501(c)(3) educational corporation organized under the laws of the state of Mississippi and granted tax exempt status by the Internal Revenue Service." (Section 3)


### Interpretations

1. (Derived from Facts 2, 4, 5, 3, 9, 10, 11) The source distinguishes between (a) underlying works created from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright (which it states can be copied and distributed in the U.S. when Project Gutenberg references are removed) and (b) distributions that are "associated with" the phrase "Project Gutenberg," which are subject to the Foundation's license, trademark, and display requirements.

2. (Derived from Facts 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14-17, 18, 19) The Foundation's Full Project Gutenberg License, as included in the eBook, requires redistributors of Project Gutenberg-associated files to include a specific notice, preserve the full license in distributed files, meet format-provision requirements for alternate formats, and meet stated refund/royalty conditions when charging fees.


### Unknowns

1. Whether and how the Project Gutenberg Foundation enforces its trademark or license terms in practice (enforcement mechanisms, frequency, or examples of enforcement) is not established by the source.

2. How the Foundation's compilation copyright in the Project Gutenberg collection affects reuse of the underlying public-domain texts when all references to Project Gutenberg are removed is not established by the source.

3. Specific legal consequences outside the United States for redistributing a Project Gutenberg-marked file (beyond the instruction to "check the laws of the country where you are located" and the Foundation's statement that it makes no representations about copyright status outside the U.S.) are not established by the source.

4. Whether a particular platform or small publisher that republishes the Gettysburg Address without Project Gutenberg references would be considered compliant in all relevant jurisdictions is not established by the source.

5. Whether the Foundation requires that the exact Project Gutenberg header and license text be preserved verbatim in all redistributions (versus allowing modified forms that still convey the required information) beyond the explicit statements in Sections 1.E.1, 1.E.4, and 1.E.5 is not further clarified by the source.


### Development sequence

1. (Fact 20) Project Gutenberg eBooks are often created from several printed editions confirmed as not protected by U.S. copyright law.

2. (Fact 2) The source states that creating works from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright means no one owns a U.S. copyright in these works and that the Foundation (and users) can copy and distribute them in the United States without permission or paying copyright royalties.

3. (Fact 19 and Fact 1) The Project Gutenberg eBook (the Gettysburg Address file) is distributed with the Full Project Gutenberg License included in the eBook and states that users may copy or re-use the eBook under those license terms.

4. (Fact 4 and Fact 3) The Foundation claims a compilation copyright in the Project Gutenberg collection and identifies "Project Gutenberg" as a registered trademark.

Relationship between steps 234: UNKNOWN (the source presents these statements but does not explicitly state causal relationships among them).

5. (Fact 5) The source states that if an individual work is unprotected by U.S. copyright and the user is in the U.S., the Foundation does not claim a right to prevent copying, distributing, performing, displaying, or creating derivative works provided all references to Project Gutenberg are removed.

Relationship between step 4 and step 5: UNKNOWN (the source states both, but does not explicitly connect them as cause/effect).

6. (Facts 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14-17, 18) The Full Project Gutenberg License includes explicit distribution requirements and constraints for works "on which the phrase 'Project Gutenberg' appears, or with which the phrase 'Project Gutenberg' is associated," including: a required prominent sentence (1.E.1); prohibitions on removing the full license text (1.E.4); a requirement to display the sentence when redistributing (1.E.5); format/ASCII-provision requirements for alternate formats (1.E.6); prohibitions on charging fees unless certain conditions are met (1.E.7); and specified royalty, refund, and compliance conditions if charging (1.E.8); and special conditions when a work is posted with permission of the copyright holder (1.E.3).

Relationship between step 5 and step 6: UNKNOWN (the source states both statements but does not explicitly state how they interact in all scenarios).

7. (Facts 6, 7, 8) The source states that copyright laws of the user's jurisdiction govern what can be done with the work, instructs non-U.S. users to check their local laws, and states that the Foundation makes no representations concerning copyright status outside the United States.


