Synthesis
---------

### Central insight
Project Gutenberg distributes many U.S. public-domain works free of U.S. copyright claims, but when a copy of a work carries the phrase "Project Gutenberg" (or is otherwise associated with Project Gutenberg) its Full Project Gutenberg License and trademark rules impose explicit redistribution obligations - including a required license sentence, preservation of the full license text, format/availability requirements, and restrictions on charging (including a 20% royalty clause) - while the Foundation states it does not claim the right to prevent copying in the United States if all references to Project Gutenberg are removed. (Research Facts: 2; 9-11; 12-14; 3; 5; 19.)

### Evidence
1. Project Gutenberg states that creating works from print editions not protected by U.S. copyright law means "no one owns a United States copyright in these works," so "the Foundation (and you!) can copy and distribute it in the United States without permission and without paying copyright royalties." (Fact 2.)
2. The Full Project Gutenberg License requires that a specific sentence (the Project Gutenberg header sentence with active links to the full license) "must appear prominently whenever any copy of a Project Gutenberg work (any work on which the phrase 'Project Gutenberg' appears, or with which the phrase 'Project Gutenberg' is associated) is accessed, displayed, performed, viewed, copied or distributed." (Fact 9.)
3. The license also instructs: "Do not unlink or detach or remove the full Project Gutenberg License terms from this work," and forbids copying or distributing the work "without prominently displaying the sentence set forth in paragraph 1.E.1 with active links or immediate access to the full terms." (Facts 10-11.)
4. The license imposes format/availability requirements: if a distributor provides the work in a format other than the official "Plain Vanilla ASCII" (or other official format), they must, at no additional cost, provide or make available the original official format upon request. (Fact 12.)
5. The license restricts charging: distributors "do not charge a fee" for access unless they comply with specified paragraphs; where charging is allowed, the license requires paying "a royalty fee of 20% of the gross profits" derived from use of Project Gutenberg works and meeting refund/defect-handling conditions. (Facts 13-16; 14.)
6. Project Gutenberg is a registered trademark, and the Foundation identifies itself as owner of a compilation copyright in the Project Gutenberg collection - distinctions the license uses when describing rights tied to the phrase "Project Gutenberg." (Facts 3-4.)
7. The Foundation states that if an unprotected work is used in the United States and "all references to Project Gutenberg are removed," it does not claim a right to prevent copying, distribution, performance, display, or derivative works. (Fact 5.)
8. The Full Project Gutenberg License is included with eBooks (the license text is part of the distributed file and/or is available online). (Fact 19.)

### What the narrative should emphasize
- Emphasize the core tension: the underlying text can be public domain in the United States (Evidence 1) while copies that carry Project Gutenberg branding are distributed under explicit license and trademark-linked obligations (Evidence 2-5, 6, 8). Those two propositions (public-domain status vs. license/trademark conditions) are the essential facts that make this story relevant to librarians, educators, and redistributors.
- Preserve the documented distinction that removing all Project Gutenberg references eliminates the Foundation's asserted right to restrict copying in the United States (Evidence 7). Present the license provisions (required sentence, retention of license text, format/availability rule, and charging/royalty conditions) as the concrete mechanics that create obligations for redistributors of Gutenberg-marked files (Evidence 2-5, 8).
- Optionally illustrate with one concrete example: the 20% royalty requirement and the mandatory prominent sentence with active links (Evidence 5 and Evidence 2) to show how the license translates into specific redistribution rules.

### What should be de-emphasized
- Do not turn compilation copyright (Evidence 6) or administrative details about the Foundation into proof that the underlying public-domain texts are owned; the compilation copyright is a distinct statement and its legal effect on reuse when Project Gutenberg references are removed is not established in the source. (Fact 4; see Gaps and uncertainty.)
- Avoid broad coverage of unrelated Project Gutenberg operational or historical details (e.g., update/renaming practices, organizational tax status) that do not change the central tension between public-domain status and redistribution terms. (Facts 21, 22.)

### Gaps and uncertainty
- The Research does not establish whether or how the Foundation enforces its trademark or license terms in practice (no enforcement examples or mechanisms are provided). (Unknown 1.)
- The legal effect of the Foundation's compilation copyright on reuse of the underlying public-domain texts when all references to Project Gutenberg are removed is not established by the source. (Unknown 2; Fact 4.)
- The Research does not establish specific legal consequences outside the United States for redistributing a Project Gutenberg-marked file; the license repeatedly directs non-U.S. users to check local law and disclaims representations about copyright status outside the U.S. (Facts 6-8; Unknown 3.)
- The source does not clarify whether the Foundation requires the Project Gutenberg header and license to be preserved verbatim in all redistributions versus allowing modified forms that still convey the required information. (Unknown 5; Facts 9-11.)

Narrative
---------

Selected Story
Project Gutenberg distributes many U.S. public-domain works that anyone may copy in the United States, but when a distributed file is labelled or otherwise associated with the phrase "Project Gutenberg" the Foundation's Full Project Gutenberg License and trademark rules place explicit obligations on redistributors - including a required prominent header sentence with active links, preservation of the full license text, format/availability requirements, and limits on charging (including a stated 20% royalty clause). The reader should understand that the legal status of the underlying text (public domain in the U.S.) is distinct from the license- and trademark-linked conditions that apply to Gutenberg-branded copies, and that the Foundation says removing all Project Gutenberg references removes its asserted restrictions in the United States.

Story Progression
- Context: For digital librarians, educators, volunteer transcribers, small publishers and platforms that redistribute public-domain texts - the underlying works (for example, the Gettysburg Address) may be public domain in the U.S. and therefore freely copyable, but Project Gutenberg distributes copies that carry its name and accompanying license text.
- Development: When a copy carries the phrase "Project Gutenberg" the Full Project Gutenberg License requires a specific prominent sentence with active links, forbids removing the full license text, imposes format/availability rules (e.g., making the official format available on request), and restricts charging for access (including a stated 20% royalty on gross profits when charging is allowed); Project Gutenberg also identifies its trademark and a compilation copyright as distinct rights tied to the Project Gutenberg designation.
- Endpoint: The central takeaway is that redistributors who work with Gutenberg-marked files must follow those license and trademark-linked obligations unless they remove all references to Project Gutenberg - a step the Foundation says ends its asserted restriction in the U.S. The source does not establish how the Foundation enforces those rules in practice or the specific legal consequences outside the United States.

Critical Constraints
- Do not present an asserted or unstated enforcement practice as fact; the source does not document how or whether the Foundation enforces the license or trademark terms in practice.
- Do not claim that the Foundation's compilation copyright or trademark gives it ownership of underlying U.S. public-domain texts when all Project Gutenberg references are removed; the source treats those as distinct rights and does not establish contrary legal effect.
- Do not assert legal consequences outside the United States or treat U.S. public-domain status as definitive worldwide; the license disclaims representations about non-U.S. copyright status.
- Do not attribute decisions, motivations, or actions to an unspecified team or collective actor; describe only the documented license terms and the Foundation's stated position.

Compose
-------

Many works are public domain in the United States, but a copy that's labeled or linked to Project Gutenberg carries separate obligations. If you redistribute a Gutenberg-branded file, those obligations travel with the file - even though the underlying text may be free to copy in the U.S.

This matters for digital librarians, educators, volunteer transcribers, small publishers, and platforms that share public-domain texts. A Gutenberg-marked file is not just the plain text of a public-domain work; it is distributed under the Project Gutenberg Full License and subject to the Foundation's trademark and compilation rights tied to that designation.

What those obligations look like:
- A required prominent header sentence with active links.
- Preservation of the full license text in redistributed copies.
- Format and availability conditions, for example making the Foundation's official format available on request.
- Limits on charging for access, including a stated 20% royalty on gross profits where charging is permitted.
The Foundation also identifies its trademark and a compilation copyright as distinct rights associated with the Project Gutenberg name.

Those license and trademark conditions apply to copies that carry Project Gutenberg references; they are separate from the underlying work's U.S. public-domain status. The Foundation says that removing all Project Gutenberg references removes its asserted restrictions in the United States.

The license also disclaims any representation about a work's copyright status outside the United States, so international copyright questions are treated separately.

Bottom line: if you're redistributing files that are labeled Project Gutenberg, the label brings specific license and trademark rules. If you distribute the same underlying text without any Project Gutenberg references, those Project Gutenberg-linked conditions are not asserted to apply in the U.S.

